- train period
- validation period
- test period
- dataset version
- feature version
- strategy/code version
- number of trials
- random seed
- OOS Sharpe
- CAGR
- max drawdown
- Calmar
- Sortino
- turnover
- trade count
- costs
- slippage
- statistical confidence
- robustness score
- information sources
- pass/fail
- promotion status

Never overwrite previous experiments.

PHASE 14 — PAPER TRADING
Only after a strategy passes the research gates.

Paper-trading system must:
- generate live signals
- record expected execution
- simulate fills
- record latency/slippage
- compare predictions with realized outcomes
- monitor data outages
- monitor feature drift
- monitor model degradation
- compare live behavior with backtest assumptions

Do not move directly from backtest to real capital.

PHASE 15 — LIVE DEPLOYMENT
Only after successful paper trading.

Start with very small capital.

Required controls:
- position limits
- max daily loss
- max portfolio drawdown
- kill switch
- API failure handling
- stale-data detection
- duplicate-order protection
- order reconciliation
- complete trade logging
- alerting

GO / NO-GO CRITERIA
A strategy is eligible for paper trading only if all of the following are satisfied:
- data-quality checks pass
- point-in-time checks pass
- no known leakage exists
- OOS evaluation is genuinely untouched
- transaction-cost stress is acceptable
- slippage stress is acceptable
- signal-delay stress is acceptable
- placebo performance is materially weaker than real performance
- uncertainty is acceptable
- performance is not isolated to one narrow regime
- implementation is reproducible
- experiment is fully registered

A strategy is NOT eligible merely because:
- in-sample Sharpe is high
- one walk-forward result is high
- the backtest return is high
- a complex model beats a simple model
- social/news features improve one experiment

RECOMMENDED IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
1. Real SPY OHLCV dataset.
2. Timestamp/availability validation.
3. Point-in-time feature engine.
4. Simple baseline models.
5. True walk-forward evaluation.
6. Costs/slippage/delay tests.
7. Placebo/bootstrap/multiple-testing diagnostics.
8. Diagnose signal vs exposure/regime effects.
9. Controlled strategy discovery.
10. Information-source ablation.
11. Portfolio construction.
12. Risk engine.
13. Research leaderboard/experiment registry hardening.
14. Paper trading.
15. Only then consider live trading.

NEXT CONCRETE DELIVERABLE
Create Institutional_Quant_Research_Engine_V2.1.ipynb from the current V2 notebook.

V2.1 must:
- default to real-data ingestion rather than synthetic data when credentials/data paths are available
- retain an explicit offline/demo mode for testing
- make data versioning mandatory
- make availability timestamps mandatory for point-in-time features
- freeze the OOS test set once a research run begins
- strengthen placebo/null diagnostics
- report model-vs-benchmark attribution
- produce the standard experiment registry automatically
- finish with a clear RESEARCH_ONLY / PAPER_READY / LIVE_READY gate

SUCCESS DEFINITION
The goal is not the highest backtest return.
The goal is a robust, reproducible, economically plausible edge that survives:
- point-in-time validation
- true out-of-sample testing
- transaction costs
- slippage
- execution delay
- regime changes
- placebo tests
- multiple-hypothesis risk
- live execution differences

Until those conditions are satisfied, the system remains a research platform only.
